          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-8367dce9eb16\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"“Je vous aime trop” directly avows an excessive degree of love and presents that emotional state as the explanation for the speaker’s jealousy and distress. It does not independently undertake a commitment, and nothing indicates that the words are a reflex-like expressive discharge rather than an emotionally charged assertion.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A speaker directly addresses Marguerite and tells her, “je vous aime trop.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal is embedded in an explanation of jealousy: because the speaker loves Marguerite “too much,” even her thoughts provoke jealousy, while the secrecy surrounding her proposed plan causes emotional pain. The utterance is therefore chiefly a causal self-description, however intense its surrounding emotion, rather than a promise or reflexive outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Marguerite, je vous aime trop”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense declaration, intensified by “trop,” strongly supports truth-conditional avowal of a loving emotional state.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“et je suis jaloux de la moindre de vos pensées”\",\"supports\":\"The coordination presents love and jealousy as asserted psychological states and makes the love avowal explanatory rather than performative.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“le mystère qui précède l'exécution de ce projet me serre le cœur”\",\"supports\":\"The speaker describes emotional distress caused by the situation, but this intensity does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is located in Chapter XIII of Alexandre Dumas fils’s La Dame aux camélias.\",\"supports\":\"Identifies the bibliographic and narrative location of the directly spoken occurrence.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core force as an avowal of love used to explain jealousy and anguish. The emotional context requires no separate E score, and there is no residual core force requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
